messages:
maria: FYI found a docs/runtime mismatch for /v1/generate-stream. Current Node + Python snippets call JSON.parse on raw chunks but server emits SSE (text/event-stream). Customers see 'Unexpected token d' errors.

kevin: Ouch. Which files?

maria: docs/snippets/node/generate-stream.md and docs/snippets/python/generate_stream.py. Customer on Dedicated hit it in prod.

sam: Quick suggested Node patch to handle SSE lines:
```js
stream.on('data', chunk => {
  const text = chunk.toString()
  text.split(/\r?\n/).forEach(line => {
    if (!line.startsWith('data:')) return
    const payload = JSON.parse(line.replace(/^data:\s*/, ''))
    // process payload
  })
})
```
Add a 1-line note: "Streaming uses SSE framing; parse 'data:' lines or use sdk.stream helper."

olga: OpenAPI currently marks response content-type as application/json. I'll open a PR to change it to text/event-stream and update examples + add migration guide.

docs-bot: PR opened: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/551 by olga changes: openapi media-type, node/python snippets, migration note. CI: docs-lint passed.

kevin: I'll add a tiny SSE sample vector in runtime tests so docs can validate snippet parsing. Ping me the expected payload.

maria: Customer can use sdk helper or non-streaming endpoint as temporary workaround. I'll attach PR link to their ticket.

sam: I'll land the snippet + migration blurb now and include a troubleshooting entry for the parse error.

olga: I'll follow up with a full migration doc at docs/migration/streaming-sse.md with curl, Node, Python examples and a QA checklist. ETA EOD.

maria: Thanks all, marking this resolved for customer unless they reopen.
